[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. It is not one issue. The
reports were merged on subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)), and
the subject says nothing about the cause. Read by tenant, channel and body, the reports split
into three problems with three different fixes.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix closes only the first row. The other two stay open after it ships.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

## The three problems

**`parser`: day and month swap.** The shared parser reads every date month first, and every
tenant on file writes the day first. 7 April arrives as 4 July. This is the bug #4471 describes,
and the queued fix is for it.

**`hint`: the booking-v3 form asks for the wrong order.** booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its
date field. People type what it asks, and the form reads the date day first, the tenant's way.
Only form reports from booking-v3 tenants land here. A correct parser does not fix it: the hint
must match the order the form reads.

**`shift`: one day early.** These reports say a date moved by one day, not that day and month
swapped: a Tuesday shown as Monday, a meeting held on two days for clients in Lisbon and Toronto,
an invite that arrived before its booking. That is a time-zone conversion. The desk noted them as
a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) and count them under the parser cause by tenant, so the
parser row includes them. They still need their own fix.

## How to read a date report

- Count unique report ids. The ticket export pages overlap, so one report can appear twice.
- The intake tag is a guess from the reporter's browser. The cause follows the tenant's locale,
  its booking form and the channel (`causes.ts`).
- Read the body before you merge. A one-day move and a swapped date share a subject line, not a fix.

